==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|houwen}}, {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|houwan}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*hawwaną}}. Cognate with English {{m|en|hew}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈhaʊ̯ən]|[ˈhaʊ̯n]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-hauen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|hau|en|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{head|de|verb|third-person singular present|haut|past tense|haute|or|hieb|past participle|gehauen|or|gehaut|auxiliary|haben}}

# {{lb|de|literary}} to [[thrust]], [[slash]] (with a weapon)
#: ''Er  '''hieb''' nach meinem Helm.'' — “He thrust for my helmet.”
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[cut]], [[hew]]
#: ''Der Einbrecher  '''haut''' ein Loch in die Wand.'' — “The burglar cuts a hole in the wall.”
# {{lb|de|colloquial}} to [[hit]], [[beat]], [[bang]], [[whack]]
#: ''Meine Mutter hat ihn '''gehauen'''.'' — “My mother hit him.”
#: ''Sie '''haute''' die Nägel in die Wand.'' — “She banged the nails into the wall”
# {{lb|de|reflexive|colloquial}} to [[fling]] oneself
#: ''Ich werde mich in die Falle '''hauen'''.'' — “I'm going to hit the sack”
# {{lb|de|regional}} to [[chop]], [[chop down]]

====Usage notes====
The strong preterite form {{m|de|hieb}} is only common in the literary sense of ''to [[thrust]]'', and also in the set phrase ''[[in die gleiche Kerbe hauen]]'', otherwise it's rare and/or stilted, with {{m|de|haute}} being used instead. However, the main past participle is still {{m|fi|gehauen}} outside of some regional vernaculars.

